1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,062
I signed up for a store card
at checkout and it quietly
destroyed 11% of my
available credit overnight.

2
00:00:08,062 --> 00:00:21,642
I was 24, had a decent 680
credit score, and thought I
was being smart building
credit history. I had two
existing cards with a
combined $6,000 limit and
almost no balances.

3
00:00:21,642 --> 00:00:49,650
The cashier said I'd save
20% on a $180 purchase, so I
signed up on the spot. The
store card came back with a
$300 limit. That tiny limit
meant even a $90 balance put
me at 30% utilization on
that card alone. My score
dropped 34 points inside 60
days because of the hard
inquiry plus that high
utilization ratio hitting at
the same time.

4
00:00:49,650 --> 00:01:14,263
First, I paid that $90
balance completely within
the next billing cycle. Then
I called my existing card
issuer and requested a
credit limit increase, which
bumped my total available
credit from $6,300 to
$9,300. I also set a $25
autopay on the store card so
it always showed a tiny
balance rather than zero or
a spike.

5
00:01:14,263 --> 00:01:21,902
Within 5 months my score
recovered to 694, and six
months after that I was
sitting at 711.

6
00:01:21,902 --> 00:01:28,267
If this sounds familiar,
drop your situation in the
comments and I'll try to
help.
